  "answer_text": "Here's an overview of **AltaVista** and what became of it:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd0d What Was AltaVista?\n\nAltaVista (meaning \"high view\" in Spanish), based in Palo Alto, California, was a web search engine. Launched in **December 1995**, it was the first \"full text\"/boolean searchable index of the World Wide Web.\n\nIt was the result of a research project started in the summer of 1995 at Digital's Research Laboratories, combining a fast Web crawler with scalable indexing software.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcc8 Rise to Popularity\n\nWeb traffic increased steadily from 300,000 hits on the first day to more than **80 million hits per day** by 1997, making it one of the most popular websites at the time. However, traffic plummeted shortly thereafter due to competition from **Google Search** and others.\n\nIn 1996, AltaVista became the exclusive provider of search results for Yahoo. In 1997, it launched **Babel Fish**, a web-based machine translation application that translated text or webpages.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcbc Acquisitions & Decline\n\nIn January 1998, **Compaq** acquired Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC) for $9.6 billion, and as part of the transaction, acquired AltaVista.\n\nIn February 2003, **Overture Services** acquired AltaVista for $140 million. In July 2003, **Yahoo** acquired Overture for $1.63 billion.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd1a The End of AltaVista\n\nIn **June 2013**, the domain name was redirected to **Yahoo Search**. Today, visiting **altavista.com** simply takes you to Yahoo's search engine.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc6 Legacy\n\nAltaVista is credited with implementing the **first CAPTCHA** to prevent fraudulent account registrations and internet bots from automatically adding pages to its web index.\n\nAltaVista was truly a pioneering force in the early internet era, helping define what web search could be \u2014 before ultimately being overtaken by Google.",
